200f6"} {"question" "q-126995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩)-⟨2,3,6⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-3t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨2,3,6⟩+⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨-1,1,7⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨5,5,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041286/state.json" "file_sha256" "33723098ec9036a058d34148050980f29b6e83d0e42fe99ed96394043cf200f6"} {"question" "q-126995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041286/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c73d23bd8bda3653a2c735d25fd3f39c83e31fe3e1d881dcf365dcf8cc58b635"} {"question" "q-126995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widge