r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041286/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "eae1034bdc4d317e8040a627962566e80e89f3c164c8d98fbc65009d00af33d6" "rationale" "The displacement Q-P is (5,2,5). Therefore r=P+t(Q-P), with real t, passes through P at t=0 and Q at t=1 and defines their line. Original option 3 has precisely this base point, direction, and domain. The stored answer agrees component by component. This is a source-backed derived judgment corroborated by the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grade."}] "usage" {"q-35982" [] "e-12442" [] "e-12443" [] "e-12444" [] "e-12445" [] "q-35960" [] "q-22291" []} "basis" 1492 "retractions" [[17592186106212 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{11}{2},-7)$ and parallel to $v = ⟨1, 1,-2⟩$."] [17592186106215 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(0,-2,-4)$ and $Q(5, 0, 1)$."] [17592186106215 :question/answer-fields 17592186103799] [17592186106118 :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3, 1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v = ⟨2, 1, 3⟩$. Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP = \\sqrt{14}$."] [17592186105386 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186105386 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105387 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7)$."] [17592186105387 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= PQ^(→) \\\\\n&= ⟨3, 2, 7⟩ - ⟨1,-1, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 3, 5⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105388 :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle$. Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}$."] [17592186105388 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= p + tv \\\\\nr &= ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^(→) &= OA^(→) - OP^(→) \\\\\n&= (⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩) - ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 4t, t, t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nPA=6\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mid PA^(→) \\mid &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mid ⟨ - 4t, t, t⟩ \\mid &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-4t)^{2} + t^{2} + t^{2}} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{18t^{2}} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n± 3t\\sqrt{2} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\nt &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\nOA_{1}^(→) = ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + 2⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ = ⟨ - 7, 4, 2⟩,; OA_{2}^(→) = ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ - 2⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ = ⟨9, 0,-2⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2)$."] [17592186105389 :question/problem "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$, where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1)$."] [17592186105389 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= PQ^(→) \\\\\n&= OQ^(→) - OP^(→) \\\\\n&= (-2i + 5j - k) - (-4i + j + 2k) \\\\\n&= 2i + 4j - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= (-4i + j + 2k) + t(2i + 4j - 3k) \\\\\n&= (2t - 4)i + (4t + 1)j + (2 - 3t)k, t ∈ [0, 1]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186102946 17592186102947 17592186102948 17592186102949 17592186102950 17592186103782 17592186103783 17592186103784 17592186103785 17592186103786 17592186103800 17592186103801 17592186103802 17592186103803 17592186103804] "immutable_fields" [[17592186102945 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102945 :answer-field/id] [17592186102945 :answer-field/key] [17592186102945 :answer-field/type] [17592186103781 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103781 :answer-field/id] [17592186103781 :answer-field/key] [17592186103781 :answer-field/type] [17592186103799 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103799 :answer-field/id] [17592186103799 :answer-field/key] [17592186103799 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
